## Context
The tournament (cycle 2, report beta 1) runs each configuration as one model
doing orchestration, coding and review inside Pi. The report names this as a
limit: IDH workflows can assign those three roles to different models, so a
ranking of single-model configurations does not say which model to put in which
seat. The reader of the report cannot act on "Luna is the best compromise" for the
coder seat alone, or for the reviewer seat alone.

The arena (`skills/arena`) therefore needs a redesign to test the three roles
separately. Related, not blocking: 0974 (closed, superseded by 1052: workers are
now chosen by the orchestrator from the route grid), 1047 (blog posts that state this limit), 1051 and 1050
(candidate scouting and long-context rerun).

## Design questions to settle first
- Unit of measure. A role-split run is a triple (orchestrator, coder, reviewer),
  so the arm count grows as the product of candidates per seat. Decide between
  a full factorial on a few candidates, one-factor-at-a-time around a fixed
  baseline, and a fractional design. Price each in USD and author-hours before
  choosing; the tournament cost is the reference.
- Scoring. Today three judges score the deliverable out of 30. A reviewer-seat
  test needs a quality measure for the review itself (defects found, false
  alarms, verdict agreement with a reference), not only the final deliverable.
- Contamination. The judge panel must exclude families that sit in any of the
  three seats under test, as the cycle-2 candidate list already requires for
  executors.
- Seed tickets. Same sampling as cycle 2 or a fresh draw; ten tickets gave
  nominal 5 % comparisons only, so a role-split design needs more tickets or
  paired comparisons to say anything per seat.
- Fixed seats. Which seats stay fixed while one varies, and which model fills
  them.
- Decorrelation. Reviewer seats are meant to differ from the producer
  (`rules/workflow.md` § Delegation); the design should measure whether that
  matters.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Written design: the measured quantity per seat, the factorial or
      one-factor plan, the judge-exclusion rule, the ticket sample, and a
      priced run budget
- [ ] Author decision on the budget and the candidate list before any run
- [ ] `skills/arena` updated, or a successor skill, to run role-split arms
- [ ] `make check` passes
